  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<2,y<1.$",
  "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14201862/assets/3911ef3923eabce680eb406a93c02aaacd2b91f829294eb7ac0e26a11753a703.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<1,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$",
